Found by building the thing that was missing: a way to run a preview batch through a REAL bridge
without the IDE (scripts/preview_batch_turn.py). Its first real run came back

    [ERROR] workspace required: open a project folder before starting a Crusor session

`prompt` is in the bridge's _WORKSPACE_REQUIRED_METHODS and the check runs per frame, before the
session is even resolved. The workbench's own composer sends one
(crusorAIRendererService.ts: `workspace: this.workspacePath`); the preview batch frame did not. So
step 9 — the entire payoff of the feature — would have died on every click of *Send to Crusor*, and
nothing in the suite could see it: the frame is assembled in TypeScript, and the refusal only
happens inside a running engine.

Two more things fell out of the same inspection:

* the service resolved its workspace with `folders[0]` — the exact pattern the contribution's P0
  rule forbids ("never silently pick folders[0]"). It now shares one `workspacePath` getter that
  declines in a multi-root workspace instead of guessing, and refuses to send with an honest log
  line rather than dispatching a frame the engine will reject.
* `resolveEndpoint` treated an unresolvable workspace as "match anything", so with no folder open —
  or in a multi-root workspace — the window could frame *another project's* proxy. It now accepts
  only endpoints whose `workspace` is one of the folders actually open.

The Python builder takes `workspace` as a REQUIRED keyword: a builder that can silently emit a frame
the engine rejects is a trap, and one call site had already walked into it. The lab and the harness
pass their workspace explicitly.

Pins, both verified to fail when the fix is reverted (checked): the contract test isolates the frame
literal inside `sendBatchToAgent` and requires the workspace to come from `workspacePath`, and the
prompt test asserts the frame carries it and that the builder refuses a blank one.

The harness itself is worth stating plainly, because it is the only end-to-end proof of this feature
that runs without Electron: it prints the exact prompt, spawns `python -m src.bridge` the way the
workbench does, streams reasoning/tool-calls/tokens, and diffs the workspace afterwards. Its own
first version hung — stderr was never drained, so the child blocked on a full 64 KB pipe; it now
drains stderr in a thread and decodes binary pipes, both lessons copied from
src/tests/test_bridge_transport.py. `--demo` edits a temp copy of the lab fixture, so nothing in the
repo is touched.

Gate A: Crusor errors: 0, 34 files in program (TS 6.0.3). 121 passed, 1 skipped.

